Take the receiver's socket for I_Rcv_Conn_Ack under the peer lock.

The process that opens a peer connection - usually the peer_timer process -
hands the socket to the peer's receiver over the fd exchange pipe and then,
back in I_Snd_Conn_Req(), stores its own descriptor number in the shared
p->I_sock. The receiver stores its copy, a different number in a different
process, in p->I_sock too, but before sm_process() and so without the peer
lock, which the timer still holds. Whenever the receiver gets there first,
the timer's number overwrites it, and I_Snd_CER() in the receiver runs on a
descriptor that is not the socket: getsockname() fails with "Error on
finding local host address > Not a socket", or the CER goes to whatever the
number is in the receiver, and the peer is left half-open in Wait_I_CEA
without a CER ever reaching it. Seen on cold compose starts about one in
three: an I-CSCF or S-CSCF that never connects to its DRA or its OCS and
stays unhealthy.

sm_process() already gets the receiver's descriptor as its sock argument,
and takes the lock, which is only free once I_Snd_Conn_Req() has returned,
so the I_Rcv_Conn_Ack transitions set p->I_sock from it.

--- a/src/modules/cdp/peerstatemachine.c
+++ b/src/modules/cdp/peerstatemachine.c
@@ -134,6 +134,7 @@
 		case Wait_Conn_Ack:
 			switch(event) {
 				case I_Rcv_Conn_Ack:
+					p->I_sock = sock;
 					p->state = Wait_I_CEA;
 					I_Snd_CER(p);
 					if(p->state == Closed)
@@ -230,6 +231,7 @@
 		case Wait_Conn_Ack_Elect:
 			switch(event) {
 				case I_Rcv_Conn_Ack:
+					p->I_sock = sock;
 					I_Snd_CER(p);
 					if(p->r_cer) {
 						p->state = Wait_Returns;
